<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.1.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00702</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.1.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b65457bf-34b1-4f71-a22e-6b2b13b71a49</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="30068c90-b089-4b92-a495-446e12f918e8" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>1. The scatter plot shows the number of times a player came to bat and the number of hits they had.</para><para><figure><media alt="A scatter plot. Horizontal, 0 to 600, by 50’s, labeled at bats. Vertical, 0 to 150, by 12 point 5’s, labeled hits. 19 dots trend linearly up and to the right."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5f26a53b3c89edd866e7f9aa2db6ccf666bf83a7"/></media></figure></para>
      <div>
        <para>The scatter plot includes a point at \((318,80)\). Describe the meaning of this point in this situation.</para>
      </div>
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! For every 318 at bats, there are 80 hits. </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para><para>The correct answer is that for every 318 at bats, there are 80 hits. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="c2210f3f-1767-4b78-a5b5-643e917e2f0d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For questions 2—5, use the following to answer each question.</para><para><quote>The scatter plot shows the number of minutes people had to wait for service at a restaurant and the number of waitstaff available at the time.</quote><quote>A line that models the data is given by the equation \(y=-1.62x+18\), where \(y\) represents the wait time, and \(x\) represents the number of waitstaff available.</quote></para><div>
        <para>The slope of the line is \(-1.62\). What does this mean in this situation?</para>
        </div>
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! The slope means that for every increase of 1 staff person, the wait time decreases 1.62 minutes.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para><para>The correct answer is the slope means that for every increase of 1 staff person, the wait time decreases 1.62 minutes. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="4d5749f2-bca8-4182-880f-b7cd08b57751" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> The slope of the line is \(-1.62\). Is it realistic to conclude, for this slope, as \(x\) increases, \(y\) will decrease?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Yes. For every increase of staff people, \(x\), the wait time, \(y\), decreases. For an increase of 1 staff person, the wait time decreases 1.62 minutes. This is realistic.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: Yes. For every increase of staff people, \(x\), the wait time, \(y\), decreases. For an increase of 1 staff person, the wait time decreases 1.62 minutes. This is realistic. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="b1a4bbef-f1a5-4164-a881-9907e963311e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,18)\). What does this mean in this situation?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! When there are 0 staff people, the wait time is 18 minutes. This is not realistic when there are no staff available.
      </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para><para>The correct answer is when there are 0 staff people, the wait time is 18 minutes. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="f696961c-25dc-45ae-9779-26781eddb56d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,18)\). Is this a realistic \(y\)-intercept point if \(y\) represents the wait time and \(x\) represents the number of staff available?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No, this is not realistic when there are no staff available.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: No, this is not realistic when there are no staff available. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="1086b591-c6a7-4c6a-a760-8716500fefc2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para> The company’s pricing plan states that the usage rate is constant for any number of minutes connected to the Internet. The number of minutes is related to the cost in dollars and has a linear relationship. The equation of the line is \(y = 0.03x +0.10\). What does the slope represent in this situation?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! For every minute of Internet usage, the price increases 3 cents. </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is for every minute of Internet used, the price increases 3 cents. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="0893a163-5d6c-4fc7-a03a-fd2f1e15aa4d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para> Below is a scatter plot of the data with two linear models, \(y=130-5x\) and \(y=25.3+3.66x\). Which of these two models does a better job of describing how shoe length \(x\) and height \(y\) (in inches) are related?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y\;=\;25.3\;+\;3.66x\)</para><para>\(y=130-5x\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The line of \(y = 25.3 + 3.66x\) is a better choice because as shoe length increases, height tends to increase.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is the line of \(y = 25.3 + 3.66x\) is a better choice because as shoe length increases, height tends to increase. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="690d7104-ba71-4fc3-9335-5e53a6db81e2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para> The scatter plot below displays the elevation and median number of clear days per year of 14 U.S. cities. Two lines are shown on the scatter plot. Which line fits the data better?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Line 2 fits the data better because more points seem to be closer to the line. </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is Line 2 fits the data better because more points seem to be closer to the line. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e7c962cd-ccb9-477a-8982-6a32387fe740" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>According to the scatter plot below, what claim can be made about women’s shoe length and height?</para>
      <figure><media alt="A SCATTER PLOT THAT SHOWS SHOE LENGTH IN INCHES ON THE X-AXIS AND HEIGHT IN INCHES ON THE Y-AXIS. "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/711fc739283a09fb281aa876602ac9371fd5be27"/></media></figure></problem><solution><para>Correct! As a woman’s shoe length increases, her height also tends to increase. </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: As a woman’s shoe length increases, her height also tends to increase. </para></solution></exercise></section>
  <para>For questions 10 – 12, use the scatter plot below.</para>
  <para>This scatter plot shows foal birth weight and mare’s weight.</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="A SCATTER PLOT THAT SHOWS MARE WEIGHT IN KILOGRAMS ON THE X-AXIS AND FOAL WEIGHT IN KILOGRAMS ON THE Y-AXIS."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8568caa2f5d1375c92bcdf0e2f9b05d77d12917d"/></media></figure></para>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="df1c0214-99ea-424d-be82-48c61718f632" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What does the slope mean of a linear model on this scatter plot?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>the increases in foal weight in kg for each kg increase in mare weight</para><para>the increase in mare weight in kg for each kg increase in foal weight</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The slope represents the increase in foal weight in kg for each kg increase in mare weight</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2 – 3.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is the slope represents the increase in foal weight in kg for each kg increase in mare weight. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="7f65c0a6-2f8f-48ea-9e26-b922072ad0e7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Will the \(y\)-intercept of a linear model make sense in this problem?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No. When the mare weight is zero, the linear model will be above 0 on the \(y\)-axis.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.5.</para><para>The correct answer is: No. When the mare weight is zero, the linear model will be above 0 on the \(y\)-axis. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="07a82a5e-01fb-47af-a9cb-8008398d1fd9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What does the point \((548, 108)\) mean in this problem?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! A mare weighing 548 pounds has a foal weighing 108 pounds.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 3.1.2 – 3.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is that a mare weighing 548 pounds has a foal weighing 108 pounds. </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
